---
## NOTES FOR CONTRIBUTORS
---
# ✅ WHAT TO COMMIT:
#   - pyproject.toml (dependencies specification)
#   - src/ (source code)
#   - tests/ (test suite)
#   - docs/ (documentation)
#   - manifests/presets/ (YAML pipeline configs)
#   - scripts/ (utility scripts)
#   - .claude/ (Claude Code customization)
#   - .gitlab/ (CI/CD templates)
#   - .gitignore, CONTRIBUTING.md, CLAUDE.md (project guidance)

# ❌ NEVER COMMIT:
#   - .env, .env.local (API keys, secrets)
#   - venv/, .venv/ (regenerate with: pip install -e .[v1,dev])
#   - __pycache__, .pytest_cache/, .coverage (build/test artifacts)
#   - .mypy_cache/, .ruff_cache/ (tool artifacts)
#   - manifests/production/ (V4+ scope, not in V1)
#   - *.log, logs/ (runtime logs)
#   - .claude.local.md (personal preferences)

# WHY SECRETS ARE GITIGNORED:
# API keys, database URLs, OIDC settings, and manifest-resolved secrets are
# user/machine-specific and MUST NEVER be committed to version control.
#
# Setup locally (each developer):
#   1. Create .env with your values (template: see .env.example if exists)
#   2. NEVER commit .env to version control
#   3. For CI/CD: use GitHub Actions secrets or the deployment platform's secret store
#
# Example .env:
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
#   QDRANT_URL=http://localhost:6333
#   QDRANT_API_KEY=...

# WHY VENV IS GITIGNORED:
# Virtual environments are machine-specific and bulky.
# Always regenerate from pyproject.toml:
#   python -m venv .venv
#   source .venv/bin/activate  # or .venv\Scripts\activate on Windows
#   pip install -e .[v1,dev]

# Windows WSL alternate-data-stream junk
*:Zone.Identifier
